						Preventing Message Truncation &amp; Mobile App Lag
					</h3>
					<div class="mt-4 space-y-4 text-[14.5px] leading-relaxed text-body">
						<p>
							When pasting massive repeated blocks into social or messaging applications, character count arithmetic is more nuanced than counting visible letters. Standard ASCII letters (<code class="rounded border border-hairline bg-canvas px-1.5 py-0.5 font-mono text-[12px] text-ink">A–Z</code>) occupy a single 7-bit or 8-bit byte. However, modern emojis (such as <strong class="font-medium text-ink">❤️</strong>, <strong class="font-medium text-ink">🔥</strong>, or <strong class="font-medium text-ink">🎂</strong>) and accented symbols are multi-byte characters encoded across surrogate pairs in UTF-16. In standard telecom SMS systems, introducing even one emoji immediately downgrades the message encoding from GSM-7 to UCS-2, shrinking single-segment capacity from 160 characters down to just 70 characters and causing long text to fragment across multiple billable segments.
						</p>
						<p>
							Similarly, while WhatsApp supports a theoretical message payload of 65,536 characters, sending an unbroken wall of 50,000+ characters or complex emoji chains can cause recipient mobile devices to freeze, stutter during DOM rendering, or fail to open chat bubbles smoothly. For social sharing, keeping repeated text sequences between 1,000 and 10,000 characters provides maximum visual impact while ensuring rapid rendering across iOS and Android clients without truncation or app crashes. You can also monitor your exact word and character footprint with our free <a href={getLocalizedPath("/word-counter/", locale)} class="font-medium text-ink underline decoration-[#0f2e26]/30 underline-offset-4 hover:decoration-[#0f2e26] dark:decoration-[#afe3d0]/40 dark:hover:decoration-[#afe3d0]">word counter</a> before sending.
